Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Parcel Clock Runs at Forty-Two and a Half
- Story & Context: The debut runs at 170 BPM, but Awa plays the main xalam note once every four beats, creating a perceived 42.5 BPM pulse. Theo builds a sparse kick-snare lattice around it, leaving one quarter-note gap before each new xalam cycle. Mamadou answers alternate phrases at full 170 BPM with quick tama rolls, then switches to one slow bend per xalam attack. Elise places one vibraphone fifth every eight bars and allows it to ring through multiple breakbeat cycles. Idris enters only after thirty-two bars with a breath-heavy contrabass clarinet root. Midway through, Awa doubles her attack rate to imply 85 BPM while every electronic event remains unchanged. The track feels as if it accelerates even though the session tempo never moves. The final section returns to quarter-speed implication and ends with one exposed xalam note after the drums stop.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A brass parcel scale beside two mechanical clock faces, one marked 170 and one marked 42.5, indigo shipping ink on cream paper, xalam strings crossing the foreground. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “forty-two and a half,” “same train,” “different clock,” and “meet at the cycle.”

Track 2: Indigo Stamp Falls Between the Ghost Snares
- Story & Context: Theo programs the EP’s most detailed ghost-snare pattern at 172 BPM, but intentionally leaves a narrow silent pocket every second bar. Awa places one muted xalam pluck inside that pocket while keeping the main ostinato at half-speed. Mamadou bends a tama stroke upward immediately after the mute, making the silence feel framed rather than empty. Elise uses a damped vibraphone minor seventh whose decay ends just before the next snare cluster. Idris plays no pitched notes for the first half, using only key clicks and breath. In the final minute, Theo removes the main snare entirely while the ghost layer remains. The listener continues to hear the absent backbeat because the xalam mute and tama bend preserve its outline.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Indigo parcel stamp suspended above a sorting card, its rectangular mark positioned between two hand-drawn ghost-snare symbols, xalam bridge and vibraphone bar in shallow focus. Spoken fragments limited to “stamp between,” “leave the snare hollow,” “ghost still counts,” and “do not fill the gap.”

Track 3: Brake Van Passes While the Calabash Stays Still
- Story & Context: The closer begins at 168 BPM with rolling but restrained break fragments and one narrow reese tone. Awa plays a two-note xalam figure so slowly that each note occupies an entire two-bar span. Elise answers with an undamped vibraphone octave, allowing its decay to extend farther than the xalam itself. Mamadou alternates between one fast three-stroke tama call and one completely silent cycle. Idris enters with a low contrabass clarinet fifth that appears only when both xalam and tama are absent. In the last two minutes, Theo progressively increases break complexity while Awa reduces her phrase until only one note remains every eight beats. The machine grows faster in detail while the acoustic center becomes more still. The recording ends with the drums cutting abruptly, leaving the final xalam resonance and room air untouched.
